(Title) Tj
T*
(Impact of telehealth support on emotional adjustment and coping in family caregivers of) Tj
T*
(residents with dementia) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: Family caregivers experience substantial psychological burden following) Tj
T*
(institutional placement of relatives with dementia. We evaluated a structured telehealth) Tj
T*
(intervention designed to facilitate adjustment and enhance coping. Methods: A total of 240) Tj
T*
(primary caregivers were randomized to receive either the Residential Care Transition) Tj
T*
(Module \(RCTM\) or control comparison. The RCTM included 6 core sessions plus flexible ad) Tj
T*
(hoc phone support over 12 months. Primary outcomes were depressive symptoms \(PHQ-9 score) Tj
T*
(change\) and perceived care-related stress assessed at months 3, 6, and 12. Secondary) Tj
T*
(outcomes included caregiver mastery and intervention acceptability. Results: At 12 months,) Tj
T*
(the RCTM group demonstrated mean PHQ-9 reductions of 3.8 points versus 1.2 points in) Tj
T*
(controls \(P = 0.015\). Caregiver-reported stress showed similar favorable patterns. Ninety-) Tj
T*
(one percent \(107/120\) of RCTM participants completed all structured sessions. Qualitative) Tj
T*
(interviews identified key therapeutic elements including validation of feelings, practical) Tj
T*
(problem-solving guidance, and sustained accessibility of a supportive clinician. Higher) Tj
T*
(utilization of flexible sessions correlated with greater symptom improvement. Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(Telehealth-delivered support effectively reduces depressive symptoms and facilitates) Tj
T*
(emotional adjustment during the dementia institutionalization transition.) Tj
